  "sections": [
    {
      "sectionId": "section-introduction",
      "heading": "Introduction",
      "sourceAnchor": "introduction",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "\n\tThe Mythal Touched feat offers a little taste of wild magic, allowing you to\n\troll on a table when target by hostile magic and produce an unpredictable\n\tmagical effect. Unfortunately, the table is almost half self-destructive\n\toptions, and very few of the options are purely beneficial making this\n\texceptionally difficult to justify.\n"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "\n\t"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "\n\t\t"
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "Mythals",
              "href": "https://www.google.com/url?q=https://forgottenrealms.fandom.com/wiki/Mythal&sa=D&source=editors&ust=1762230330162713&usg=AOvVaw3mn7OyYk_2BKRf4L0z27di"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "\n\t"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "\n\tare powerful magical effects which influence how magic works in a large area.\n\tThink of them like magically-enforced laws banning certain things (creatures,\n\tschools of magic, etc.) and empowering others (magical light effects, etc.).\n"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "\n\tThe only reason I would take this feat is on a joke character. Combine this\n\twith Wild Magic Sorcerer or Wild Magic Barbarian and lean into the nonsense of\n\trandom tables. I would consider this character absolutely miserable to play at\n\tlength, but it could be fun in a low-stakes one-shot.\n"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-ability-score-increase",
      "heading": "Ability Score Increase",
      "sourceAnchor": "ability-score-increase",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-ability-score-increase-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Any mental stat. Easy for casters."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-mythal-ward",
      "heading": "Mythal Ward",
      "sourceAnchor": "mythal-ward",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-mythal-ward-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "\n\tHow exactly is this a “ward”? 11 in 20 results (just over half) don’t hurt\n\tyou, but the others are harmful both to you and to nearby creatures. Unless\n\tyou’re within 15 feet of your attacker, you’re likely to hurt only yourself\n\tand your allies. Even if you’re close to your enemy, it’s hard to justify this\n\tunless you’re close to multiple enemies so that you can apply the negative\n\teffects to multiple enemies.\n"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-mythal-ward-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "\n\tThis is dangerous, unpredictable, self-destructive, and rarely useful in a way\n\tthat you’ll actually enjoy. Exactly 5 of the 20 results on the table are\n\tpurely beneficial. Are you really going to spend a feat and a Reaction to have\n\ta 1 in 4 chance for something nice to happen? Of course not.\n"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
